# core/coverage-gaps - Which elements of this type lack an expected property?
#
# Answers: elements of RESOURCE_TYPE with no EXPECTED_PREDICATE asserted.
#
# Does NOT prove: that the real thing lacks the property. It proves nobody
# recorded it. "No owner is modelled" and "this system has no owner" are different
# statements, and reporting the second when you measured the first is the most
# damaging mistake available here.
#
# Before reporting a gap, check three things. That RESOURCE_TYPE actually has
# instances - a type with zero members produces zero gaps and looks like perfect
# coverage. That the predicate spelling is right - use core/element-detail on one
# element you know carries it. And which models are in scope, since a gap
# concentrated in one model is usually a partial export, not an ownership problem.
#
# THIS QUERY IS DATASET-WIDE, both when finding instances and when deciding that a
# property is absent, and neither is a stylistic choice. This template used to scope
# both to the semantic graph, which was wrong in two opposite directions at once:
#
#   Nothing found. Since the 1.3 layout `arch:Model` lives in `graph/model`, so a
#   semantic-only scope cannot see a single model. Asked which models lack a source,
#   it reported zero gaps - the answer that means "perfect coverage" for a type it
#   never looked at. That is the exact failure the first paragraph warns about,
#   produced by the query rather than by the reader.
#
#   Gaps that are not gaps. A graph-local negative test only proves the property is
#   absent from the graph the type happened to be asserted in. Model provenance lives
#   in `graph/provenance`, so in fixtures/base.trig four of five models carry
#   `dct:source` there while being typed in `graph/model`: scoping the absence test
#   would have called all five a gap, and only one of them is.
#
# So "no value" here means no value anywhere in the dataset, which is the only reading
# under which absence is evidence that nobody recorded it. The independent scopes -
# `any2`, `any3`, `any4` - matter for the same reason: sharing one graph variable would
# require the label, the membership edge and the type assertion to sit in the same
# graph, and where they do not the column silently arrives empty.
#
# DISTINCT because a type assertion repeated in more than one graph is one resource,
# not two, and a duplicated row inflates a count that reads as a population.
#
# The model comes from {{MEMBERSHIP:element}}, and it is the ONE thing here that stays
# inside the resource's own scope rather than spanning the dataset. That is not an
# inconsistency: under `same-graph-colocation` the pattern is "?model is a model", which
# only means "this resource's model" when it is evaluated in the resource's own graph.
# Rendered in a dataset-wide scope it would match every model in the store and name the
# wrong one - the failure that mode's documentation warns about.
#
# Read the column with the type you asked about in mind. RESOURCE_TYPE is caller-chosen
# and need not be a model concept: ask about arch:Model itself and membership is
# meaningless, so under the direct-predicate mode the column is simply unbound, which is
# the right answer to "which model does this model belong to".
#
# Parameters: RESOURCE_TYPE, EXPECTED_PREDICATE, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT DISTINCT ?element ?label ?model
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:any}}
    ?element a {{RESOURCE_TYPE}} .
    OPTIONAL { {{MEMBERSHIP:element}} }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:any2}}
      ?element {{EXPECTED_PREDICATE}} ?anyValue .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:any3}}
      ?element {{PATH:label}} ?label .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
ORDER BY ?model ?label ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
